Grade 1 gastrocnemius strain from stepping off something awkwardly to calf
Minor Trauma and Personal Injury
Focal medial calf tenderness, Pain on active plantarflexion, Normal Thompson test

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MTI-005
Reason for Visit: Sudden sharp calf pain causing ambulation difficulty, requiring assessment for Achilles rupture or deep vein thrombosis.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified sudden acute calf pain mimicking Achilles tendon rupture. Urgent assessment was required to clinically exclude complete tendinous rupture or acute vascular events, preventing profound morbidity before the next routine clinic appointment.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I stepped down awkwardly and felt a sharp pull in the back of my calf, and now it hurts to walk.
History of Present Illness: Patient reports a sudden sharp twinge and pulling sensation in the posterior lower leg during an eccentric load while stepping off a step. There is mild tightness and a cramping sensation in the calf exacerbated by active plantarflexion against resistance. The patient maintains the ability to bear weight and walk, presenting with a minor antalgic limp favoring the forefoot.
Functional Status: Ambulating independently but with an antalgic gait.
Social/Safety Concerns: Unsteady gait marginally increases risk of slips or falls.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 130/84 | HR: 76 | Temp: 36.7 | RR: 14 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 78 Kg
General: Alert, non-toxic, mildly uncomfortable when mobilizing.
Physical Exam: Lower limb examination reveals focal medial calf tenderness localized to the medial head of the gastrocnemius. Palpation demonstrates mild localized induration without a palpable defect, gap, or massive swelling. Pain is reproduced on active plantarflexion and passive ankle dorsiflexion. Intact Achilles tendon continuity is confirmed by a normal Thompson calf squeeze test (plantarflexion observed). Sensation and distal pulses in the foot remain entirely normal.
Environmental Observations: Safe, single-level living environment.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Grade 1 gastrocnemius strain - Status: Stable
Differentials:

Achilles tendon rupture - Ruled out due to: Normal Thompson test and absence of a palpable tendinous gap.

Deep Vein Thrombosis (DVT) - Ruled out due to: Clear traumatic mechanism of injury, absence of entire leg swelling, and localized mechanical tenderness.

Soleus strain - Ruled out due to: Tenderness isolated specifically to the medial head of the gastrocnemius rather than deeper tissues.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The abrupt onset of sharp posterior calf pain required immediate bedside differentiation between a simple muscle strain and an Achilles tendon rupture. Clinical assessment successfully confirmed a structurally intact Achilles tendon and focal gastrocnemius strain. Urgent after-hours assessment was clinically indicated to rule out surgical emergencies (Achilles rupture) and acute vascular pathologies (DVT), ensuring safe, conservative weight-bearing parameters could be established immediately rather than risking further injury overnight.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed topical NSAID gel and oral paracetamol. Advised against aggressive stretching in the acute phase. Bedside musculoskeletal ultrasound recommended via standard referral to confirm muscle fiber integrity if recovery stalls.
Positive Behavioral Change: Insert a small heel raise in the footwear to place the gastrocnemius in a slightly shortened position. This reduces resting mechanical tension, directly limiting pain and promoting cellular healing.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): Heel wedges/lifts for footwear.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 1 week for progression to active rehabilitation.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Attend the Emergency Department if the entire lower leg becomes red, swollen, and hot, or if you develop sudden shortness of breath or chest pain.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is based on clinical presentation; soft tissue injuries require dynamic monitoring.
Clinical Course: Acute pain typically subsides over 7 to 10 days. Full return to explosive or high-impact activities may require 4 to 6 weeks of graduated rehabilitation.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate emergency attention if your whole calf rapidly swells up without reason, or if you suddenly cannot point your toes downwards at all.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: A heel lift decreases the stretch on the torn muscle fibers, preventing repetitive microtrauma during walking.
Actionable Steps: Walk with a slight heel wedge in your shoes. Avoid forceful pushing off with the toes. Apply ice for 15 minutes after walking.

